## Device / platform support

[FILL IN — see `CLAUDE.md`'s "Device / platform support" section for
the standing rule. Document here: which platforms are targeted, the
concrete `isPad`-style (or equivalent) device-idiom-check pattern used,
and where it lives.]

### Orientation lock (if the app has any layout mode that needs a non-default orientation)

A reusable pattern for an app where most of the UI is locked to one
orientation (typically portrait) but one specific mode/screen
legitimately needs another (e.g. a split-screen or landscape-specific
layout) — skip this whole subsection if every screen in the app is
happy with a single fixed orientation everywhere.

**iPhone (or any non-adaptive-multitasking platform)**: keep the
default fixed-orientation lock everywhere *except* the one mode that
needs it. A static flag (e.g. `AppDelegate.allowsLandscape`, kept in
sync with the relevant feature toggle by the composition root) backs
the orientation-lock delegate method
(`application(_:supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor:)` on iOS), returning
the wider orientation mask only while that flag is set. The flag's
`didSet` should immediately re-query the OS
(`setNeedsUpdateOfSupportedInterfaceOrientations()`) rather than waiting
for the next rotation attempt, and — if the special mode is turned off
while the device is currently held in the now-disallowed orientation —
actively rotate back
(`UIWindowScene.requestGeometryUpdate(_:)`), so the device doesn't stay
sideways showing a layout that was never designed for it.

**iPad (or any platform where users generally expect free rotation)**:
skip the lock entirely — check the device idiom first in the same
delegate method and short-circuit to allow every orientation,
regardless of the iPhone-only flag above. Also skip the forced-rotate-
back-to-portrait behavior on this platform specifically; it would
otherwise fight "always allow every orientation" the moment the special
mode is toggled off. This isn't just cosmetic — a layout that was
tightly tuned for one orientation on the locked platform may need
separate tuning to actually look right once the same layout is allowed
to rotate freely on a platform with more screen space (see
`docs/subscription-paywall-pattern.md`-adjacent note in
`LESSONS_LEARNED.md` #3–#4 for what goes wrong if that tuning work
isn't kept properly scoped to the platform it's meant for).

Remember the static declaration counterpart this needs in
`project.yml` — see `LESSONS_LEARNED.md` #2. The runtime mechanism
above and the static `Info.plist` declaration are two separate
requirements; both are needed.

