## Forced-update kill switch

[FILL IN, or keep as-is if reusing the pattern verbatim — this one
generalizes cleanly:]

A remote safety valve independent of App Store review turnaround: two
Remote Config values, `minimum_app_version` and `app_store_url`. A small
`AppVersion` Domain type does real `major.minor.patch` comparison (not
lexical string comparison — `"1.10"` sorts before `"1.9"` as plain
strings). The comparison is exposed as a pure static function taking
both versions as arguments, so it's testable without needing to control
`Bundle.main`.

Gate lives at the **root** of the app's `WindowGroup` content (kill
switch checked before anything else, since it's unresolvable from inside
the app), as a plain view swap — not a sheet/`.fullScreenCover`, which
could carry an interactive-dismiss gesture that undermines "no bypass."
The block screen has no cancel/dismiss affordance, only an "Update Now"
button opening `app_store_url`.

Make `isUpdateRequired` a **computed** property, not decided once at
launch — if the underlying Remote Config sync already re-runs on
foreground resume, this re-evaluates live too, blocking an
already-running session on its next foreground resume rather than only
at the next cold launch. Default `minimumAppVersion` to `"0.0.0"`
(permissive) until the first successful fetch, so a device that's never
synced (including a first-ever launch that happens to be offline) is
never incorrectly blocked.

This has no dependency on StoreKit or the Apple Developer Program — pure
Remote Config plus a version comparison plus a blocking screen, fully
testable at any signing tier.

## Legal clickwrap + consent

[FILL IN — see `docs/legal-compliance-checklist.md` and the
`legal/*.template` files for the concrete content pattern. Architecture
shape:]

A versioned acceptance gate: a `LegalDocumentVersion` enum with one
constant per document/consent type (e.g. `.terms`, `.privacy`, and any
additional one-time consent this app's core feature legally requires —
e.g. a recording-consent constant for an app with a voice-capture
feature). Bumping a constant is what forces re-acceptance — compare the
stored accepted version against the current constant, not a separate
"needs re-accept" flag.

Terms/Privacy clickwrap extends the kill-switch's view swap to a 3-way
gate (kill switch → legal acceptance → main content) at the app's root.
Host the actual legal text externally (Firebase Hosting or equivalent)
rather than bundling it in the app binary, so it can be updated without
an app release and so there's one canonical copy shared between every
in-app link and whatever URL is registered in the App Store listing.

Any additional one-time consent specific to the app's core feature
(e.g. recording consent for a voice app, location consent for a
location-based app) should be its own gate, checked at the point the
feature is first used rather than at launch — shown once, not re-shown
per use, unless the core interaction model specifically requires
otherwise (weigh this against real UX cost: gating a rapid, repeated
interaction behind a fresh consent tap every time will break the
product for no real legal benefit over a one-time notice that stays
reachable from Settings).

**Scope note**: legal text is typically scoped to the launch
jurisdiction(s) at first — [FILL IN: which] — and does not address
every jurisdiction's requirements (e.g. GDPR Article 27 representative
for the EU). Revisit before expanding distribution.

## Dependency direction

```
Presentation  →  Domain  ←  Data
```
Data and Presentation both depend on Domain (protocols + models);
neither depends on the other directly. The composition root
(`[FILL IN: App]App` / its root view's `init`) is the *only* place
concrete `Data/` types get named — that's the only file that needs to
change to swap a vendor or point tests at fakes.

## Testing

`[FILL IN: App]Tests/` has mock implementations of every Domain protocol
and one focused test file per store/ViewModel. `[FILL IN: the concrete
Data/ service types]` are deliberately untested directly — the protocol
is what Domain/Presentation get tested through, not the third-party SDK
call itself; exercise those manually (StoreKit Testing's simulated
purchase flows, for example, aren't something `xcodebuild test` can
drive deterministically — see `docs/subscription-paywall-pattern.md`).

None of the unit-test suite should touch the network, real hardware
(microphone/camera/location), or the real Keychain — that's the direct
payoff of depending on protocols instead of concrete types. See
`LESSONS_LEARNED.md` #11 for the specific footgun to avoid (a test
helper's default parameter falling back to a real, not fake,
implementation).

